Subject: A kept group that changes is compared with the one before it, not asked of the shadow register by register

When a draw's program is not the last draw's, its kept groups (the stages' configuration, each stage's registers,
the vertex fetch, the link, the blend) went through the register shadow whole: some 350 lookups in a hashed table,
each its own line of memory, to find the few dozen registers that differ. On the phone that table is as cold as
the rest between two draws.

The cursor knows the words the stream last held for each group. For a group that is plain -- register packets
alone, no register twice, not HLSQ_INVALIDATE_CMD -- those words are what its registers hold, so the next group's
words are compared with them: a register both write is written when the two words differ, the shadow not asked;
one the last group did not write is the shadow's to say and to learn; one the last group wrote and this one does
not goes back to the shadow. Two groups of the same packets are compared word for word. The shadow is brought up
to date (Cursor::settle) before a draw written the long way asks it about every register, and the tests' check
compares against a copy so settled.

What this stands on -- that a kept group's registers are written by no other group of a draw -- is held to for every
program at the time its state is made (buildState): the kept groups, the pass's own and the ones made anew for each
draw are encoded and their registers compared; a program that fails is drawn the long way, always.

The streams are the same: golden three ways, the random frames, both chips. Host, A610 constants, least of 36
frames, us a draw: T 2.96 -> 2.77, K 3.19 -> 2.94, U 9.23 -> 8.36; with 16 MiB touched before each draw T 10.96 ->
10.46, S 14.90 -> 13.78 (cpu-tools/ab-host.sh; the machine was busy: the least frame is the number).


